+# Editorial review — Movement 002, chapters 9–15
+
+## Review status and coverage
+
+This is a fresh-context simulated reader review inside an informed editorial pass. It is not a blind cold read and not a real human read: the seven chapters, movement brief, book map, state ledger, canon evidence, formula instructions, supplied measurements, and supplied source-overlap result were available. Coverage is chapters 9–15 only, in order, totaling 35,432 measured prose words. The book is incomplete, so book-level progression placement, supporting-cast distribution, and final POV allocation remain provisional.
+
+**Disposition: focused repair.** The movement is strong enough that its scene design, fight development, emotional turns, and ending pressure should remain intact. One canon contradiction must be fixed, and the primary sentence-formula drift merits a controlled sentence-by-sentence repair. A small clarification to the late Havel braid would improve one-pass flow.
+
+## Unscored reader response
+
+Interest catches immediately in chapter 9 when Cael realizes the river-academy man has studied him and decides to offer the expected lock at a price of his own. The paid forearm strike and the answer that comes out of the lock form a complete learning-fight payoff, not merely another win. The movement becomes emotionally compelling when Lira names what she wants in chapter 10, refuses Cael's page, and then accepts public loss as the price of finding her own tools. Her fifth-exchange win over the clock is the movement's high point because six weeks of bruises produce a capability neither she nor Cael knew she possessed.
+
+Interest slips mildly during the extended method explanation in chapters 12–13: the six phases, performed commitment, gaze ration, Force specialist, rereading of old books, and rebuilding of the notebook are all useful, but several consecutive scenes explain variations of the same insight before the narrative advances. The chapter 14–15 Havel braid also asks the reader to replay his approach to the market from the other side without an immediate time cue, then revisits the pen tilt and pear again in the closing cutaway. The mirroring is meaningful, but the repetition slows the final turn.
+
+Lira matters most in this movement. Her desire is specific, costly, and independent of Cael; she refuses the best help he can offer because it would interfere with the thing she needs to learn. Cael remains deeply attachable through restraint: he does not pry into her night work, does not steal her fight into his notebook, and eventually recognizes that Ardenmere is the first home he chose. Havel earns interest quickly as a decent, exact man confronting the first gap between being correct and being careful.
+
+The next expected event is that the unnamed large watcher will identify himself and turn the observational method back on Cael in a direct contest. Havel's unsigned line also promises a later administrative return without prematurely explaining it. I would continue.
+
+## Reader lenses
+
+Scores are editorial judgments, not audience measurements. Confidence describes confidence in the located judgment, not market certainty.
+
+| Dimension | Fluent thirteen-year-old | Adult genre reader | Exact passage location and reason | Confidence |
+|---|---:|---:|---|---|
+| Opening pull | 8 | 8 | Ch. 9, lines 3–37, from Vell's six lines through “The river-academy man was waiting for the lock.” A concrete opponent and information disadvantage arrive before system explanation. | High |
+| Keep reading | 9 | 9 | Ch. 13, lines 79–99, the stranger entering the ten-degree blind spot; ch. 15, lines 225–277, the unsigned middle line. Both give local payoff while opening distinct pressures. | High |
+| Interest / freshness | 9 | 9 | Ch. 10, lines 201–221, Lira puts a pie lid over Cael's page; ch. 13, lines 153–247, Cael rebuilds the book around what a fighter is made of. Observation becomes character conflict rather than a generic power ladder. | High |
+| Clarity / flow | 7 | 8 | The fights are exceptionally trackable, especially ch. 14, lines 51–149. The main drag is the unmarked rewind from Havel seeing Cael at the end of ch. 14 to Red Cap's earlier warning at ch. 15, lines 3–55, plus the repeated post-visit interpretation at ch. 15, lines 205–277. | High |
+| Character attachment | 9 | 9 | Ch. 10, lines 53–105, Lira names her ambition and accepts Cael's diagnosis; ch. 15, lines 111–165, Cael discovers that “I like it here” is more than a safe answer. Both choices expose vulnerability without sentimentality. | High |
+| Humor / warmth | 8 | 8 | Ch. 10, lines 193–221, the pie lid and burned mouth; ch. 12, lines 175–181, the chestnut man's hands; ch. 14, lines 153–167, the betting man's new square for “don't know.” Humor stays character-specific. | High |
+| Action / suspense | 9 | 9 | Ch. 9, lines 127–173, the priced hit and counter; ch. 11, lines 27–107, Lira harvesting Dravin's openings while losing; ch. 14, lines 51–149, the half-beat counter-rhythm. Position, adaptation, cost, and opponent response remain legible. | High |
+| Progression payoff | 9 | 9 | Ch. 9, lines 145–207, the lock buys a whole commitment; ch. 12, lines 55–103 and 141–191, phases become a usable but priced gaze; ch. 14, lines 55–149, Lira's losses yield matched pace. Effort changes capability and consequence. | High |
+| Connection | 9 | 9 | The Movement 1 landing-beat rule pays off in ch. 9; Lira's “step that doesn't stop” develops through chs. 10–14; the sweep/officials page supports controlled findability in ch. 15; Keth is seeded in chs. 10 and 12 without forcing the future bout. | High |
+| Read-aloud quality | 7 | 8 | Speaker attribution and physical referents are clean, including multi-person rope scenes. The repeated short declaratives and repeated explanatory constructions create a more uniformly clipped cadence than the formula requests; measured mean 12.34, median 9, and only 1.5% at 40+ words support the audible impression. | High |
+
+## Continuity, canon, and story findings
+
+### Required correction — prior `[SHATTERED]` files
+
+Chapter 14, line 189 says Havel has seen the classification “five times before in four years,” and chapter 15, line 237 says he “had handled five files of this class.” This conflicts with `universe/UNIVERSE_BIBLE.md`, “The Fractured Path”: Cael is the fifth recorded `[SHATTERED]` case; there are only four prior cases in recorded history, and all four were eliminated within weeks. The wording also implies several recent passive-monitoring subjects, which those deaths make impossible.
+
+This can be repaired without weakening the scene. Havel may know the four historical cases from archived exemplars, training, a manual, or another canon-compatible source, but he cannot have handled five earlier live files. Preserve the important deduction: Cael's file is inexplicably thin for the classification.
+
+### State, mechanics, and causality — pass
+
+- Cael begins after Movement 1's twelfth Copper formal and reaches thirteen after the river-academy win (ch. 9, lines 185–189), consistent with the ledger ruling.
